# =============================================================================
# OVERWATCH JOBS — the same two jobs every crontab tier schedules
# =============================================================================
# The sweep runs hourly at minute 30, as in the crontab tiers. The daily
# OVERWATCH wake runs at 03:30 local time: pseudocron has no hour field, so
# the job carries its own hour guard (pseudocron runs every command through
# bash -c, so the test is ordinary shell).
30 scripts/overwatch-sweep.sh >> logs/cron-overwatch-sweep.log 2>&1
30 [ "$(date +%H)" = "03" ] && scripts/wake-batch.sh --agent=overwatch --sleep=0 --max-tasks=1 >> logs/cron-overwatch.log 2>&1

# =============================================================================
# MAINTENANCE JOBS
# =============================================================================
# Note: pseudocron fires a job every time the matching minute occurs within
# each hour.  Unlike cron, it has no day-of-week or hour field.  The cleanup
# jobs below fire once per hour at the listed minute.  For weekly/daily
# semantics, use a wrapper script that checks the current date/time before
# running, or schedule these jobs via cron alongside pseudocron.

# --- Cleanup script (fires hourly at minute 0; use a date-guard wrapper
#     for weekly semantics equivalent to crontab's "0 4 * * 0") ---
#     With no --project, cleanup.sh runs once per project registered in
#     projects.cfg; add --project <name> to clean a single project.
# 0 scripts/cleanup/cleanup.sh >> logs/cron-cleanup.log 2>&1

# --- Log cleanup (fires hourly at minute 30; use a date-guard wrapper
#     for daily semantics equivalent to crontab's "30 4 * * *") ---
# 30 scripts/cleanup/cleanup_logs.sh >> logs/cron-cleanup-logs.log 2>&1
